studio-config.ts described the workflow in its own comment: "the desk pastes
it back into /floor-studio and sees the floor". That is literally what
happened. /api/leads appended the Floor Studio share code verbatim into the
free-text `notes` field, an estimator found it in an email, selected it, and
pasted it into the address bar. PG0 verified there are ZERO uses of
decodeStudioDesign anywhere under app/admin/.

It worked. It is the kind of working that stops the first time somebody
edits a note, forwards a truncated email, or hires a second estimator — and
every quote that came out of the studio cost a copy, a paste and a context
switch before the one thing the homeowner most wants to talk about.

THE COLUMN, AND WHY IT IS THE CODE AND NOT AN EXPANSION OF IT

`QuoteRequest.designCode` is additive, nullable and indexed by nothing,
because nothing queries it — it is read one row at a time on an admin screen.
It stores the CODE, not a rendering of the design: expanding it here would
mean a second description of a floor living in a table that knows nothing
about the catalogue, which is exactly how two surfaces start describing one
floor differently. The admin screen decodes it against the LIVE catalogue, so
a floor we have retired resolves to nothing rather than to a confident
description of something that cannot be bought.

The note is still written too. It is what a human reads in the email
notification, and removing it would take the floor out of the one place the
desk sees a lead before opening the admin screen.

SPECIES STOPS BEING RETYPED

`QuoteRequest.species` is `Json?` holding `string[]`, and until now it was
populated only by an admin typing into the quote builder — the same admin who
had just read the species out of the note. It is now seeded from the design at
creation, when it is necessarily empty, so an estimator who edits it later is
never overwritten. `convertQuoteToProject` already copies it forward, so the
species reaches the project without anyone typing it twice.

EVERY OLD LEAD STILL WORKS

Leads written before this carry the code only inside `notes`, and there are
real ones. The fallback reads them rather than declaring them unreadable. A
backfill migration was the alternative: it would have had to parse the same
prose, once, with no way to check its work afterwards.

The parser takes the first line carrying the prefix and stops at the newline,
because `notes` is free text an estimator types into and reading to the end of
the field would fold their comment into the code and decode nothing.

WHAT THE CARD DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO

It does not render the floor. The renderer needs the visitor's own room photo,
which this business does not have and by design never will — Floor Studio
reads photos in the browser and retains none. A picture here would have to
invent a room, and an estimator looking at an invented room before a call is
worse off than one looking at a specification. A test asserts the card
contains no image element at all.

It does not restate the estimate as a fixed figure, and it prices through
`priceConfiguration` — the same function the visitor saw, in the currency
their band set was written in. An estimator quoting against a different number
than the one on the customer's screen is the argument that row exists to
prevent.

A code that will not decode renders as a stated failure with the raw code
kept, not as a blank space. Blank reads as "no design"; this lead had one and
we can no longer say what it was, which is a different thing an estimator
needs to know before the call.

Verified: 65 of 66 guards (verify:css needs postcss from node_modules, absent
here). Migrations guard clean — 27 models, 18 enums, 10 migrations, no drift.
parse-scan 611 files, zero diagnostics. Resolving typecheck clean across all
five changed files. 25 new assertions, including a contract test that the
prefix this module parses is the prefix /api/leads writes — a silent
disagreement there would look identical to "that old lead had no design",
which is the kind of wrong answer nobody goes looking for.

